A||||9||84Bill||2.5 Exhaust Dilemma||||5||
Z||000000||84Bill||06-08-2001||11:40 PM||||Over the last few months my exhaust has gotten louder and louder. So tonight I decided to get in there and check things over.I expected to find a crack in the usual spot but I was startled at what I found. (Pictures 1 & 2 ) My question is regarding picture #3 the shield. It came off my 88 2.5 and since there was not one on the 84 and it still had the over manifold dip stick, would I be safe in assuming that the recall was not done here and this shield should be installed? If not should I install it anyway?<P>Thanks<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/exhaust/image002.jpg"> <BR>if you look close you can see a crack has started on the other side.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/exhaust/image004.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/exhaust/image006.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||84Bill||06-09-2001||10:13 AM||||Orief, Ogre, Anyone? I have to get this back together before 1 today.||||reg||5
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||06-09-2001||11:32 AM||||Probably a little late now but when I had to replace mine on my 87 coupe I never had the shield and it did go in for the recall. I never had problems even after I went to a Hooker Tri-Y header. The times that we did have 4 cyls in the shop I don't recall too many having that particular sheild.<P>To answer you question, there are a great majority of Fiero 4 cyls without this sheild. If you put it on it would only be for your piece of mind.<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 06-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierokid87||06-09-2001||02:06 PM||||When I still had my 4 cyld in my Fiero I had that same heat shield.  And I know for a fact that it is on other 4 cylds Ive seen.  <P>Scott||||reg||10
Z||000004||84Bill||06-09-2001||02:45 PM||||Welp job is done. I went and got new spring bolts, gasket, hitemp antisieze, and doughnut. about 40 bucks all told. MAN what a difference in sound! It may be psychosematic but the car seems to have a bit more power now. I did a catectomy about a month ago and it sure sounds sweet now.<P>BTW I put the shield in place. I found a thread where one of the guys had it removed and the all knowing ones said to put it back on. I figured that it is there for a reason anyway. I had to remove the firewall shield since it was chafing through my A/C lines! I'll rebend it and reinstall it later.||||reg||14
Z||000005||84Bill||06-09-2001||02:48 PM||||Crap! I forgot to mention I put in the other dipstick (replaced the over manifold one) and now I'm about a 1/2 quart low! Would it be ok to fill it or should I get another dipstick?||||reg||5
Z||000006||theogre||06-09-2001||09:03 PM||||This kind of cracking is very common. It appears to be mainly from having water dumped on the manifold when hot. The automatics also have the problems from the tranny mounts described in the torque strut article.<P>You'll want to get another manifold. The weld may not hold long or the manifold will simply crack in another location. This way you have on on hand when it fails...<P>The shield did part of it's job... it kept that exhaust off the block.<P>Exactly what dip stick did you put on it? See if the part # is stamped into it.<P>The post recall stick should come up near the thermostat housing.<P>Your exact oil capacity depends on which filter is used. On mine, with the long PF52/xxx3980 filter I use 4 quarts.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||84Bill||06-09-2001||10:03 PM||||I did not see a # on the tube but the dipstick tube and stick came from another 2.5 the was in another 84 this is one that comes up to the side of the filler neck.<P>I had a newer manifold ready to go and the shield was from my 88 so I stuck it on my 84 since it had none.||||reg||14
Z||000008||qwikgta||06-10-2001||12:07 AM||||I just put a header on my 2.5  When I was under the car I said, "look at all this stupid sheetmetal around the stock manifold".  So I removed it all to give it a cleaner look.  Is that bad??  Should I put the sheetmetal sheld back on.  <P>**This is a for real question, it reads like im joking but I am not**<P>ROb<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||06-10-2001||12:40 PM||||The sheild prevents oil getting on the exhust from a valve cover or head gasket leak, or worse a block crack.<P>The sheild may not be compatible with the header. It depends on if yours looked like the one in the above picture... See how the middle port is? that would cause a leak between the header tubes. I seem to recall tho that there is a version of the sheild that has 2 holes there instead of one. If that's the case, then I'd keep the sheild.<P>As far as the dip stick.... <P>I'd change the oil and see where 4 quarts comes to on it. (Don't forget to start the car to fill the filter...) this will tell you for sure if you're low or the dipstick is off for that engine...<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-10-2001).]||||reg||1
